# Netlify serves a real file whenever one exists, and falls back to this rule
# when none does. Every route the site has now ships as a real file — the
# build prerenders them — so this rule only ever catches URLs that aren't
# pages: old links, typos, scanners.
#
# It used to point at /index.html, which was right when index.html was an
# empty shell and JavaScript decided what the page was. Now index.html IS the
# homepage, markup and all, so sending an unknown URL there would serve the
# homepage's words and the homepage's canonical tag under a nonsense address —
# and Google would be told that address is the homepage.
#
# /not-found.html is the same app rendered on its "we couldn't find that
# service" route, carrying noindex and no canonical. Same 200, same screen a
# visitor gets today; it just stops claiming to be a page that exists.
/*    /not-found.html   200
